WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.876
Authentication identifies the requester
under a particular token policy.

2
00:00:03.876 --> 00:00:06.008
Authorization decides whether that
requester may

3
00:00:06.008 --> 00:00:08.673
perform this operation on this resource
now.

4
00:00:08.673 --> 00:00:12.137
A valid access token may carry subject,
tenant, roles,

5
00:00:12.137 --> 00:00:15.068
and scopes, but none of those facts
automatically

6
00:00:15.068 --> 00:00:18.444
grants access to every object reachable
through the API.

7
00:00:18.444 --> 00:00:22.175
Enforce permissions where the requested
action and resource are known.

8
00:00:22.175 --> 00:00:25.018
A read scope may allow an API operation

9
00:00:25.018 --> 00:00:28.393
while resource ownership still restricts
which records are visible.

10
00:00:28.393 --> 00:00:32.835
Role checks may need to account for tenant
membership or workflow state.

11
00:00:32.835 --> 00:00:35.766
Apply the narrowest permissions
appropriate to the operation,

12
00:00:35.766 --> 00:00:38.431
including background actions triggered by
a request.

13
00:00:38.431 --> 00:00:40.741
Tenant scoping belongs in resource access.

14
00:00:40.741 --> 00:00:43.584
The conceptual lookup combines the
requested project identifier

15
00:00:43.584 --> 00:00:46.604
with the tenant from the validated
authentication context.

16
00:00:46.604 --> 00:00:49.446
This prevents a lookup from treating an
authenticated

17
00:00:49.446 --> 00:00:52.111
request as permission to search all
tenants.

18
00:00:52.111 --> 00:00:54.243
The route still needs its required

19
00:00:54.243 --> 00:00:56.908
scope and any ownership or business-rule
checks.

20
00:00:56.908 --> 00:00:59.040
A missing or inaccessible result should

21
00:00:59.040 --> 00:01:01.350
use the application's consistent
disclosure policy.

22
00:01:01.350 --> 00:01:04.193
A signed tenant claim is usable only if

23
00:01:04.193 --> 00:01:07.035
the trusted issuer is authorized to assert
that

24
00:01:07.035 --> 00:01:10.055
relationship and its meaning matches the
application's contract.

25
00:01:10.055 --> 00:01:12.898
A request-body tenant identifier does not
gain authority

26
00:01:12.898 --> 00:01:15.563
from being submitted with a valid token.

27
00:01:15.563 --> 00:01:19.205
For users permitted to switch tenants,
validate the selected tenant

28
00:01:19.205 --> 00:01:22.936
against authorized membership before using
it as a data boundary.

29
00:01:22.936 --> 00:01:25.601
Authorization claims also have a freshness
limit.

30
00:01:25.601 --> 00:01:28.444
Removing a role or disabling an account
may

31
00:01:28.444 --> 00:01:31.819
not change claims in an already issued
access token.

32
00:01:31.819 --> 00:01:34.395
Short lifetimes reduce that delay, but
critical

33
00:01:34.395 --> 00:01:36.883
actions may require current session or
account

34
00:01:36.883 --> 00:01:39.725
state, revocation checks, or another
authoritative decision.

35
00:01:39.725 --> 00:01:42.301
Not every request needs a database call:

36
00:01:42.301 --> 00:01:45.055
stable, low-risk permissions may tolerate
token-bound freshness.

37
00:01:45.055 --> 00:01:47.898
Decide the consistency requirement
explicitly and test cross-tenant

38
00:01:47.898 --> 00:01:51.540
access, insufficient scopes, ownership
restrictions, and changed account state.

